<!--
  Prefixed `med_alarm_` so nothing here can collide with a colour the app defines.

  DARK BY DESIGN, NOT BY THEME. This screen exists to appear at night, and a
  full-brightness white rectangle at 06:00 is its own kind of alarm. It therefore does
  not follow the device's light/dark setting — unlike every other surface in the app.

  KEPT IN STEP WITH `darkColors` IN src/theme/index.ts, BY HAND. These are Android
  resources compiled into the module at build time; they cannot import the TypeScript
  palette. When that palette moves, these move with it — otherwise the one screen the
  patient sees at 06:00, half awake, looks like a different app from the one she used
  the night before. That is a small thing everywhere else and not a small thing here.

  Contrast, measured against `med_alarm_bg` (#262220):
    fg      #F2EEE7  → 12.8:1   (body and the medicine name; WCAG AA asks 4.5)
    muted   #C3BBB1  →  7.7:1   (the time and the dose line)
    accent  #5FD3BC  →  8.5:1   (the "I took it" button fill)
  and `on_accent` #052019 on that accent → 7.6:1.

  Every pair clears AA comfortably, which is the intent: this is read by an elderly
  patient without her glasses, in the dark, under time pressure.
-->
<resources>
    <color name="med_alarm_bg">#262220</color>
    <color name="med_alarm_surface">#34302B</color>
    <color name="med_alarm_fg">#F2EEE7</color>
    <color name="med_alarm_muted">#C3BBB1</color>
    <color name="med_alarm_accent">#5FD3BC</color>
    <color name="med_alarm_on_accent">#052019</color>
</resources>
